#import ".render/designlib.typ": *
#let title = [Fabric]
#let accent = "violet"
#let body = [
#section(title: "Foundation", lead: "Typed business decisions and explicit effect ownership govern execution.", body: [

#goal(title: "Run bounded typed agents and conditional graphs")[Applications combine models, typed tools, human decisions and external work while retaining useful outcomes across process loss. Agents choose tool calls; graphs implement application-owned control protocols.]
#goal(title: "Preserve the meaning of interrupted work")[A restart retains accepted results and recorded routes. Effects that may have happened require evidence before execution can continue.]
#no-goal(title: "Retrieval and remote agent platforms")[Retrieval, vector stores, document loading and splitting remain external tools. Remote Agent Protocol execution and a general middleware chain are outside Fabric.]
#no-goal(title: "General channel graphs and automatic compensation")[Exact LangGraph APIs, time travel, arbitrary runtime code installation, shared sibling writes, quorum joins and general channels are outside the graph contract. Saga owns independent workflow compensation.]
#no-goal(title: "Distributed Erlang and directory power-loss durability")[Shared database leases coordinate capable stores. Fabric does not coordinate through Erlang distribution or promise durable directory entries across operating-system crashes and power loss.]
#invariant(title: "Effects follow confirmed ownership transitions", enforcement: "mechanism")[The runner releases an effect only after the corresponding record transition and required family grant are confirmed. Revision checks serialize writes; incarnation and attempt checks reject stale reports.]
#invariant(title: "Unknown outcomes retain their evidence", enforcement: "mechanism")[An uncertain effect blocks further dependent progress. Cancellation and expiration close business routing while retaining already performed results and unresolved child or external-job cleanup.]
#invariant(title: "Approval applies to the exact current action", enforcement: "mechanism")[Answers match run, action or activation, requirement and approval revision. Approval rechecks current policy with fresh context and accepts proofs only from the configured verifier.]
#invariant(title: "Business callbacks honor their declarations", enforcement: "convention")[Selection, acceptance, codecs and request preparation are pure. Replay declarations truthfully identify effects safe to repeat; application version changes accompany changes of meaning.]
#principle(title: "Keep authority at the owner")[Fabric owns execution and its durable records. Applications own domain state, authorization choices, truthful effect classification and resource supervision. Provider and protocol packages retain their own contracts.]
#principle(title: "Bind native values before collecting operations")[Each tool and node retains its own input and output relationship. Heterogeneous collections hide that relationship behind a bound invocation rather than a universal application value.]
#principle(title: "Share proven mechanisms without erasing lifecycles")[Agent and graph controllers share stores, fencing, supervision, family budgets and approval semantics. They retain different control and result models. #adr(1) #adr(2)]

])
#pending-ledger(
 pending-entry(title: "Streaming agents retain unresolved lifecycle scope", kind: "build", adr: [#adr(8)])[Map llm_wire progress to agent progress with explicit approval interruption points, partial transcript commits, backpressure, cancellation and stream closure. No public agent progress stream implements this scope.],
 pending-entry(title: "Whole-run time and family token bounds remain unbuilt", kind: "build", adr: [#adr(8)])[Per-call bounds and graph wait deadlines do not implement a whole-agent elapsed deadline. Per-agent observed tokens do not implement a shared family token ledger. Clock, approval pauses, child inheritance, restart and cleanup semantics require a decision before these become APIs.],
 pending-entry(title: "Approval edit and respond need continuation semantics", kind: "build", adr: [#adr(8)])[Approve, reject and expiry are implemented. Editing an action or responding with a substitute value remains retained intent; neither may reuse an approval for changed arguments.],
 pending-entry(title: "Historical forks and reducer equivalence need a ruling", kind: "ruling", adr: [#adr(8)])[Intended scope includes historical review forks and typed DAG reducer correspondence. Graphs use serial state and isolated forks, while their public scope excludes time travel. Resolve this scope tension and effect ownership before claiming reducer equivalence or admitting review forks.],
 pending-entry(title: "Suspension inside Saga needs an owned continuation", kind: "build", adr: [#adr(8)])[The Saga tool recipe handles bounded workflow reports and settlement. A suspending Fabric run inside Saga and durable external-child compensation authority remain separate integration contracts. No bridge package is selected.],
 pending-entry(title: "Queue-driven sweeper ownership remains unspecified", kind: "ruling", adr: [#adr(8)])[A Grind job can carry a run reference. Replacing independent store recovery with queue-driven scheduled wakeups requires an explicit sweeper ownership contract. Existing transactional delivery can bridge application acceptance to invocation without replacing Fabric recovery.],
 pending-entry(title: "Child context and direct recovery need targeted evidence", kind: "verify", adr: [#adr(8)])[Legacy intent asks for narrower typed child context and direct child recovery with consistent parent attachment. Current agent delegation shares the context type; graph context factories have a different contract. Verify the direct public child path before claiming that registered root recovery closes every case.],
 pending-entry(title: "Context compaction remains deferred intent", kind: "build", adr: [#adr(8)])[Specify application-visible compaction or summarization, provider replay compatibility and stored transcript meaning. Exhaustion presently stops the run and never silently discards context.],
)
#section(title: "System at a glance", lead: "One durable authority serves two control models and explicit external ports.", visual: diagram(altitude: "L1", viewpoint: "runtime", title: "Application composition around Fabric", caption: [Violet nodes belong to Fabric. External owners keep their independent lifecycles. The database adapter is a separate package, not a second core control authority.], flow: "left-to-right", nodes: ((id: "app", label: "Application", kind: "external-system"), (id: "agent", label: "Agent controller", kind: "component", tint: "violet"), (id: "graph", label: "Graph controller", kind: "component", tint: "violet"), (id: "store", label: "Store and runners", kind: "component", tint: "violet"), (id: "provider", label: "llm_wire", kind: "external-system"), (id: "tools", label: "Business effects", kind: "external-system"), (id: "backend", label: "Backend port", kind: "component", tint: "violet")), edges: ((from: "app", to: "agent", relation: "call", label: "model and tools"), (from: "app", to: "graph", relation: "call", label: "state and routes"), (from: "agent", to: "store", relation: "call", label: "confirmed transitions"), (from: "graph", to: "store", relation: "call", label: "confirmed transitions"), (from: "store", to: "backend", relation: "call", label: "CAS and leases"), (from: "store", to: "provider", relation: "call", label: "bounded model work"), (from: "store", to: "tools", relation: "call", label: "fenced effects"))), body: [
A #term("term-run") carries durable state; #term("term-context") supplies live application authority.


#points([An agent owns a transcript and action batches. A graph owns typed application state, committed activation receipts and chosen routes. Neither is a queue.], [The store supervises live runners. An idle approval or wait is data; dropping a handle does not stop its run.], [The application owns HTTP clients, authenticating verifiers and external services. Fabric does not start or stop a supplied http_gun client.])
#md-table(3, ([*Boundary*], [*Authority*], [*Contract*], [Provider], [llm_wire], [Wire interpretation, schema admission and provider replay data. Fabric owns run admission and attempt budgets.], [JSON], [json_blueprint], [Native typed boundary codecs. Schema requirements apply to model declarations and typed answers, not to every internal value.], [Observations], [sinal], [Synchronous delivery or explicitly selected bounded forwarding. Observations do not decide execution.], [PostgreSQL], [fabric_postgres], [Separate leased backend with application-owned pool. Its nested layer owns SQL and schema details.], [Saga / Relay / Warden], [Application recipe], [Workflow evidence, MCP translation and authenticated proof wiring remain explicit. Core imports none of them.]))
#adr(3) #adr(4)

])
#section(title: "Whole model and identities", lead: "Logical work, execution ownership and observation correlation have separate identities.", visual: diagram(altitude: "L2", viewpoint: "domain-model", title: "Run family and committed evidence", caption: [Each edge states ownership or retained data. Child records and the budget ledger remain separate CAS aggregates.], nodes: ((id: "definition", label: "Deployed definition", kind: "value-object"), (id: "root", label: "Root run", kind: "entity", tint: "violet"), (id: "ledger", label: "Family ledger", kind: "entity", tint: "violet"), (id: "actions", label: "Actions or receipts", kind: "value-object", tint: "violet"), (id: "child", label: "Child run", kind: "entity", tint: "violet")), edges: ((from: "definition", to: "root", relation: "dependency", label: "interprets versioned data"), (from: "root", to: "ledger", relation: "dataflow", label: "declares one budget"), (from: "root", to: "actions", relation: "dataflow", label: "retains 0..n outcomes"), (from: "root", to: "child", relation: "dataflow", label: "owns 0..n attachments"), (from: "child", to: "root", relation: "dependency", label: "one checked parent"))), body: [
A #term("term-definition-version") declares meaning. An #term("term-incarnation") fences lost-owner reports, while #term("term-correlation") only joins observations.


#entity(id: "run-record", title: "Run record", description: [The durable aggregate serializing one execution.], kind: "aggregate", owner: "Fabric controller through store CAS", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "execution")[
 #attribute(name: "Identity", type: "RunId", provenance: "authored")[The caller selects a stable id before start. Existing input under another definition or original input is a reuse conflict.]
 #attribute(name: "Definition", type: "DefinitionId and graph manifest", provenance: "authored")[Deployed code and native codecs interpret the retained data.]
 #attribute(name: "Revision and incarnation", type: "Positive integers", provenance: "derived")[The backend revision serializes commits. Incarnation fences work from a lost owner.]
 #attribute(name: "Correlation and root", type: "Correlation and RunId", provenance: "derived")[The first accepted correlation survives reopening; exact root follows validated ancestry.]
 #relates(cardinality: "1 : 0..n")[A run contains action records or graph receipts and may own many child attachments. Every child has exactly one retained parent.]
]
#entity(title: "Definition", description: [An immutable configuration retaining typed callbacks and boundary codecs.], kind: "value-object", owner: "application", lifecycle: "immutable", domain: "authoring")[#attribute(name: "Name and version", type: "DefinitionId", provenance: "authored")[Name is nonblank and version positive. A new meaning needs a new version.]]
#entity(title: "Receipt", description: [One accepted graph result and its route.], kind: "value-object", owner: "graph controller", lifecycle: "immutable", domain: "graph")[#attribute(name: "Result and route", type: "Encoded input/output/state, Next or Finished or Stopped", provenance: "observed")[The immutable record of an accepted observation governs later progress.]]
#entity(title: "Proof", description: [Evidence of an authenticated requirement check.], kind: "value-object", owner: "configured Approvers instance", lifecycle: "immutable", domain: "approval")[#attribute(name: "Verifier, requirement and lifetime", type: "Verifier identity, Requirement and time", provenance: "observed")[The configured verifier asserts authentication and authorization. The runtime checks issuer identity, requirement equality and proof age.]]
#entity(title: "Family ledger", description: [The monotonic reservation aggregate for a root family.], kind: "aggregate", owner: "budget CAS", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "admission")[#attribute(name: "Claims", type: "Work and child grants", provenance: "derived")[Validated claims reconstruct usage. A missing initialized ledger is data loss, never fresh capacity.]]
#md-table(3, ([*Identifier*], [*Meaning*], [*Reuse rule*], [RunId], [1–128 ASCII letters, digits, hyphen or underscore], [parse_id validates external text; new_id uses 128 random bits.], [ActionId], [Model turn and provider call id], [Provider call id alone can repeat; action references also name the run.], [Graph activation / attempt], [Visit / authorized replay], [New cycle visit changes activation. Replay preserves activation and increments attempt.], [Parent], [AgentParent / GraphParent / GraphBranch], [Tag keeps action and activation meanings distinct.], [Approval reference], [Run, original work identity, requirement and approval revision], [Stale and already answered requests cannot authorize another action.], [Signal reference], [Run, activation, attempt and signal definition], [One committed wait; duplicate acknowledgement compares exact result bytes.], [Correlation], [Observation join value], [Independent of idempotency; first keyed start keeps it.]))
#info(title: "Application-derived ids need canonical framing")[run.id_from_parts joins ordinary fixed-shape parts with hyphens and documents that one part containing a hyphen may alias several parts. It hashes invalid or long joined ids. Invocation and action idempotency keys use framed tuple encodings before hashing. Applications must preserve their established mappings and avoid treating correlation or attempt as logical identity. #adr(3)]

])
#section(title: "Agent authoring and model port", lead: "A pure configuration is validated before a run starts.", body: [
An #term("term-agent") retains its model, tools and native answer contract.

#subsection(title: "Typed construction")[
#answers(title: "Agent definition", responsibility: [A Spec retains name/version, model, bound tools, explicit policy, typed final answer, sub-agent declarations and all run bounds.], interface: [agent.new creates Spec(context, String); with_answer gives its answer codec. Value-first settings retain configuration. agent.build returns the validated Agent or every ConfigError.], interactions: [Tool registration checks duplicate names and schema availability; model adapters receive the same admitted input schemas. Sub-agent construction checks identities and bounds.], invariants: [Building performs no effects or process allocation. A validated Agent is the only agent definition accepted by start, open and recover.], failure: [Invalid names, versions, duplicate tools, missing schemas, unsupported answer/configuration and out-of-range limits remain construction failures. Runtime policy can still require approval without an approver; every answer is then refused.])

]
#subsection(title: "Model requests and conversation")[
#answers(title: "Model port", responsibility: [A Model is a plain application callback over a request and typed reply. Fabric owns transcript evolution and attempt accounting.], interface: [model.Request carries run, turn, correlation, system prompt, messages, tool declarations and optional answer schema. Replies distinguish final answer, tool request, refusal and truncation.], interactions: [fabric/llm adapts llm_wire caller-owned conversations and propagates correlation per turn. A deterministic callback supplies the same port for consumers and tests.], invariants: [Assistant provider data and ordered call/results survive record encoding. Missing usage remains absent. Tool-call ids must correlate exactly within the returned batch.], failure: [Opaque ModelError preserves retry classification and provider delay. Only unreachable, timed out, rate limited and overloaded are retryable; malformed requests/replies and crashes are host outcomes. Refusal and output truncation retain separate run outcomes.])

#answers(title: "Lossless history storage", responsibility: [Restore application-owned message values without coupling conversation storage to execution records.], interface: [fabric/history.codec returns json_blueprint Codec(List(model.Message)). Its independent envelope carries format fabric.history.v1 and messages. Each message uses a closed tagged union; all object fields are required, with explicit null for absent replay metadata.], interactions: [Applications compose the codec into their own native records and transactions. It serializes every message list, including empty lists and partial generated suffixes. input.new separately validates conversation relationships before admission.], invariants: [Restoration preserves message and call order, roles, text, raw arguments_json, call ids and names, provider_id, provider_state and assistant ProviderData format/value. Opaque strings remain uninterpreted; absence differs from a present empty string. The history format evolves independently of execution-record versions.], failure: [Malformed shapes, tags, types, missing fields, unknown fields and unsupported format versions fail decoding rather than silently dropping information. Blueprint owns schema composition, typed codec errors and configurable JSON parsing limits.])
#behavior(title: "Stored history restores exact message values", area: "History storage", level: "boundary")[#given[An application retains any ordered message list, including a partial turn.] #when[The application serializes and restores that history.] #then[Every message field and its order remain identical, including opaque replay data and explicit absence.] #then[Restoration grants no conversation admission or execution authority.]]
#behavior(title: "Unreadable history refuses restoration", area: "History storage", level: "boundary")[#given[Stored history has an unsupported format or a malformed, missing or unknown field.] #when[The application restores that history.] #then[Restoration returns an error without yielding a partial message list.]]
#adr(14)

]
#subsection(title: "Validated original input")[
#term("term-original-input") belongs to one run.
#answers(title: "History-aware agent input", responsibility: [Admit application-owned history as context for one new agent run.], interface: [input.new(history, prompt) returns an opaque Input or a typed InputError. input.messages exposes its validated ordered messages. fabric.start_with_input accepts Input; fabric.start preserves the single-prompt interface. fabric.matches_initial compares an Input against the stored original input. fabric.generated_messages returns only messages appended by this run.], interactions: [Construction validates the complete message sequence before appending the current UserMessage exactly once. The stored initial boundary separates imported history and current prompt from generated messages. Recovery retains that boundary.], invariants: [Original-input comparison preserves ordered message roles, text, tool-call arguments and results, and opaque provider replay data. It excludes generated messages, correlation, live context and credentials. Imported calls and results grant no tool execution or approval authority.], failure: [Malformed roles, unmatched or duplicate call results, unresolved historical calls fail at input construction. Opaque provider data remains uninterpreted by the generic input boundary. An older writer that cannot retain imported history refuses admission before model or tool effects.])
#points([Applications own conversation storage, turn selection and domain rules. Input is one execution value rather than a reusable conversation resource.], [Historical assistant answers spend no current answer attempts. The new run's model, tool, policy, token and answer bounds apply only to its new execution, including after recovery.], [Generated messages retain their exact order, call/result relationships and provider replay metadata. Fabric's existing answer API separately decodes and validates the final native answer; generated-message extraction introduces no second decoder.])
#behavior(title: "Historical calls carry no current execution authority", area: "Agent input", level: "boundary")[#given[Validated original input includes a completed historical tool exchange.] #when[A new run starts or recovers from that input.] #then[The historical exchange remains model context.] #then[Only newly admitted actions can execute under current policy and bounds.]]
#behavior(title: "History does not spend fresh answer attempts", area: "Agent input", level: "boundary")[#given[Original input includes earlier assistant answers.] #when[The new run receives an invalid final answer before or after recovery.] #then[Only answer attempts generated by the new run determine whether a corrective attempt remains.]]
#adr(13)

]
#subsection(title: "Attempt and token bounds")[

#md-table(3, ([*Bound*], [*Default*], [*Semantics*], [Model attempts], [8], [Initial call, continuations, retries and corrective answers each consume an attempt.], [Concurrent tools], [4], [Per-agent bodies; idle approvals consume no body slot.], [Model call], [600 seconds], [Fabric bounds custom models as well as llm_wire calls.], [Tool body], [60 seconds], [Timeout may leave an uncertain effect; a tool may choose a different Timeout.], [Result], [1 MiB], [Bound on serialized tool result accepted for retention and future turns. It is not a promise to stop a handler allocating a larger native value.], [Policy / command], [5 seconds each], [Separate bounded callbacks and runner command waits.], [Sub-agents / depth], [4 / 1], [Per-run delegation count and levels below root.], [Approval], [7 days], [Stored absolute expiry; older no-deadline records keep none.], [Answer attempts], [2], [One corrective final-answer turn by default, subject to remaining turn/token capacity.], [Tokens / family budget], [Opt-in], [Unreported provider usage is unverifiable, never zero.]))
#points([Admission refuses tools whose result cannot reach another permitted model turn. Exhaustion retains outstanding work without authorizing new effects.], [Retry backoff starts at 200 ms and doubles within its bounded factor. Provider Retry-After is capped at ten minutes; cancellation interrupts the wait.], [Per-run token totals accumulate observed input/output usage over every reply. A configured token limit with missing usage ends as UsageUnreported.])
#adr(4)

]
#behavior(title: "Every attempted call spends a model turn", area: "Agent budgets", level: "interface")[#given[A run has a finite positive attempt budget.] #when[A model call is attempted, including a retry or corrective answer.] #then[Its attempt count increases.]#then[Exhaustion prevents another call or tool effect whose result would require that call.]]
#subsection(title: "Typed final answers")[
#answers(title: "Final-answer validation", responsibility: [The agent validates a model final answer using its retained native codec.], interface: [with_answer changes the public answer type through Agent, Run, Status, Snapshot and child completion. Plain agents complete with String.], interactions: [The provider receives an admitted schema. Fabric validates final answers after tool use even when the provider claims schema compliance. Non-object answers may be wrapped for provider constraints.], invariants: [Completed contains the decoded native answer. Invalid content never masquerades as a completed business value. A corrective turn retains the refused answer and useful validation reason.], failure: [AnswerInvalid retains final raw text and reason when answer attempts or other budgets cannot fund another correction. A new incompatible reader codec can expose a previously stored answer as invalid; effects remain performed.])

]

])
#section(title: "Agent lifecycle and action batches", lead: "The controller emits effect descriptions; the runner commits them before execution.", body: [
Each #term("term-action") retains an individual outcome inside the agent batch.


#state-type(id: "agent-phase", title: "Agent controller phase", variants: ("awaiting-model", "acting", "stopping", "ended", "never-started"))
#entity(id: "agent-execution", title: "Agent execution", description: [The agent-specific state of a run record.], kind: "aggregate", owner: "agent controller", lifecycle: "stateful", domain: "agent")[
 #attribute(id: "phase", name: "Phase", type: "Agent controller phase", provenance: "derived", state-type: "agent-phase", state-machine: "agent-lifecycle")[Controls model admission, action settlement and terminal evidence.]
 #attribute(name: "Transcript and history", type: "Ordered messages and ActionRecord values", provenance: "observed")[Current batch and older actions retain provider data, approvals, replay counts and child links.]
]
#state-machine(id: "agent-lifecycle", subject: "agent-execution", state-field: "phase", state-type: "agent-phase", title: "Agent control across replies and stopping", initial: "awaiting-model", accepting: ("ended", "never-started"), states: ("awaiting-model", "acting", "stopping", "ended", "never-started"), transitions: (("awaiting-model", "acting", "tool request"), ("awaiting-model", "awaiting-model", "retry or answer correction"), ("awaiting-model", "ended", "answer, refusal, truncation or exhaustion"), ("acting", "awaiting-model", "all results model-visible"), ("acting", "stopping", "cancel or host failure"), ("awaiting-model", "ended", "cancel without active tools"), ("stopping", "ended", "tasks stopped and children settled")), caption: [Never-started is an independently inserted child tombstone when cancellation wins before creation. It is vacant execution, not a successful model turn.])
#subsection(title: "Per-action states")[

#md-table(2, ([*State family*], [*Meaning and transition*], [Queued / Running], [Allowed waiting slot / confirmed body-start fence. Recovery may recheck queued work; running work becomes uncertain unless declared replay-safe.], [AwaitingApproval], [Requirement, revision and optional expiry; approve rechecks policy. Changed requirements revoke the earlier permission and issue a new question.], [Succeeded / ToolFailed], [Native success or truthful Explain failure rendered to the model.], [Denied / Rejected / InvalidArguments / UnknownTool / LimitReached], [Model-visible refusal without a started body.], [Uncertain / Reconciled], [Uncertain stops continuation. Reconciliation accepts the original result once.], [Delegated / ChildSettled], [A child owns execution / terminal child evidence settles a finished parent's delegation without resuming it.], [NotStarted / Faulted], [Withdrawn before effects / host could not retain or complete the action.]))
#answers(title: "Action batch", responsibility: [Coordinate independently typed calls from one assistant reply while retaining call order in the transcript.], interface: [ToolCall values, ActionId(turn, call_id), per-action ActionState, answered approvals, replay counts and optional child id.], interactions: [Decode arguments before policy. Allowed calls enter a bounded executor; each result commits independently. Other calls may finish while one requires approval or reconciliation.], invariants: [A report must name the expected action and current ownership. Repeated or foreign reports cannot progress state. The next model turn waits until every necessary action is model-visible.], failure: [Codec and policy faults are host failures. Unknown tools, invalid arguments, truthful business failures and explicit denials are visible observations. Uncertainty never becomes an ordinary model-visible failure automatically.])

]
#subsection(title: "Recovery and retained results")[

#points([A lost model task can start another model attempt under remaining budgets. Already accepted assistant turns and tool results are reused.], [A queued approved action asks for approval again after ownership loss because its checked context was live and is unavailable. A stored answer grants no later incarnation authority.], [A started action without result retains uncertainty unless its explicit replay promise permits a bounded restart. Replayed tool count is retained.], [A child's approvals and uncertain actions appear through the parent family. Public commands route references to their named child rather than inventing a parent action.])

]
#behavior(title: "A partial batch retains independent outcomes", area: "Agent recovery", level: "interface")[#given[Some tool results are committed and another body has started.] #when[The owning runner is lost and the run is recovered.] #then[Committed results remain recorded.]#then[The interrupted body is uncertain unless its retained replay contract permits re-execution.]#then[No earlier successful tool is repeated solely because the batch was incomplete.]]

])
#section(title: "Tools and effect settlement", lead: "Typed application handlers supply truthful failure and replay classification.", body: [
The #term("term-effect-fence") precedes the body. An #term("term-uncertain-effect") requires #term("term-reconciliation") or authoritative #term("term-settlement").

#subsection(title: "Tool definition and binding")[
#answers(title: "Typed tool boundary", responsibility: [A Definition owns tool name, description and input/output codecs. Binding retains the typed handler and its application error classifier before collection as Tool(context).], interface: [define, bind and bind_settling; handlers receive live context, tool.Call(run, action, correlation) and decoded input. Definition accessors serve consumer wiring and policy.input.], interactions: [The same definition supplies the model declaration and execution decoder. Generic handler errors become Explain or Uncertain only at this boundary; they need no persistence error codec.], invariants: [Input authorization may additionally occur inside the handler. A model name or schema is not authentication. A declaration is not effect permission.], failure: [Explain asserts a definite business failure; Uncertain asserts an effect may have happened. Handler crash, task loss and timeout conservatively retain uncertainty. Output encoding faults after an effect do not permit retry.])

]
#subsection(title: "Replay and cancellation")[
#answers(title: "Effect contract", responsibility: [Keep at-most-one local admission distinct from external effect guarantees.], interface: [with_replay explicitly authorizes bounded re-execution and with_timeout sets the tool body bound. idempotency_key is stable for logical run/action.], interactions: [Executor obtains a confirmed start fence before the body. Cancellation stops local tasks and the controller waits for declared late settlement or child outcomes.], invariants: [Neither a timeout nor a read-only annotation proves a mutating operation safe to replay. Downstream idempotency, transaction or fencing must uphold the declared contract.], failure: [Stop before the fence is definite NotStarted. Stop after the fence can remain Uncertain even when the local task no longer runs. Terminal cancellation preserves effect history.])

]
#subsection(title: "Late settlement window")[
#answers(title: "Settlement port", responsibility: [Accept the eventual result of an effect whose local invocation task stopped.], interface: [bind_settling supplies Settlement and a finite settle_within. settle reports confirmation, AlreadyRecorded, NotAwaited or an unconfirmed store outcome.], interactions: [A stopping run first confirms its executor stopped. A settlement offered while the task may still act waits within the bound; settlement from its own task is refused immediately. A running run with an uncertain action can accept a later definite settlement.], invariants: [The first valid settlement or reconciliation wins. A terminal settlement adds evidence without restarting a model or graph successor.], failure: [Too-late settlement leaves a person responsible for uncertainty. An uncertain settlement cannot resolve an already uncertain active action. Lost storage acknowledgement must not be described as accepted settlement.])

]
#behavior(title: "Cancellation retains unknown external effects", area: "Effect lifetime", level: "interface")[#given[A body may have acted and its outcome is unresolved.] #when[Cancellation or expiration closes execution.] #then[Business continuation is suppressed.]#then[Unresolved evidence remains inspectable and reconcilable.]#then[A stop request or local task exit does not claim the remote effect stopped.]]
#adr(2) #adr(5)

])
#section(title: "Policy and authenticated approvals", lead: "Permission is checked against exact work and fresh live context.", body: [
#term("term-policy") decides current admission. #term("term-approval") names a pending question; #term("term-proof") supplies authenticated evidence for its answer.

#subsection(title: "Admission decisions")[
#answers(title: "Policy gate", responsibility: [Authorize each agent call/delegation and graph activity, signal, job, owned job, child or fork admission.], interface: [Policy(context) receives Action(run, Step, name, arguments_json, Target), with decoded input represented by its original codec. Decision is Allow, Deny(reason) or RequireApproval(Requirement).], interactions: [Application chooses policy; always_allow is explicit. Agent denial becomes a model-visible observation; graph denial becomes a failed graph. External policy callback is bounded.], invariants: [Policy failure and invalid requirement fail closed. Visibility of advertised tools and permission to execute remain different application decisions; no general dynamic visibility policy is supplied.], failure: [Policy error, exception or timeout never substitutes Allow. A changed runtime configuration is not permission to reinterpret already retained arguments.])

]
#subsection(title: "Proof identity and reviewer audit")[
#answers(title: "Approver port", responsibility: [Authenticate and authorize one credential for a Requirement without forcing Warden into Fabric.], interface: [approvers.new(name, verify), check and with_proof_lifetime. Verifier returns a typed Reviewer or NotAuthenticated, NotAuthorized or Unavailable. Proof is opaque.], interactions: [Application constructs Approvers once at boot and shares that exact value between runtime and answer handler. Warden recipe validates audience and application-owned approve:<name> scope convention.], invariants: [A proof must come from the configured instance, match the exact requirement and remain within the 60-second default lifetime. It is not single-use because the durable answer already commits once.], failure: [NoApprovers, OtherApprovers, OtherRequirement and ProofExpired refuse the answer without mutation. The verifier callback runs synchronously in check; its author must bound network calls. A proof lifetime is not an automatic callback deadline.])

]
#subsection(title: "Answer and expiry semantics")[

#points([Approve rechecks current policy and uses the same fresh context for the admitted action. Denial or policy failure wins. Another requirement returns RequirementChanged and a new question.], [Reject requires a matching proof and records the reviewer, verifier and reason. It denies the existing action; it grants no later permission.], [Approval expiry uses store-clock time and retained absolute due time. Agent expiry is a model-visible rejection; graph expiry is ExpiredApproval failure. Old records without due time remain unbounded.], [Wrong, stale, duplicate, ended and expired references have distinct typed outcomes. Answering cannot consume a different activation or command. Unavailable clock or write confirmation admits no effect.])
