#section(title: "Signals and durable wait deadlines", lead: "Waits retain correlation data and release execution capacity.", body: [
A #term("term-signal") is input to one committed wait, whose deadline survives recovery.

#subsection(title: "Typed signal consumption")[
#answers(title: "Signal port", responsibility: [Accept one typed external value for a committed wait and atomically apply its graph decision.], interface: [signal.new(versioned output contract), operation.await_signal(input codec), graph.deliver and deliver_json. SignalReference carries run, activation, attempt and contract.], interactions: [Policy gates publishing a wait. Waiting holds no executor body or permanent runner. The application authenticates/authorizes a signal caller independently of the reference.], invariants: [Delivery validates original saved contract and deployed codec. Receipt, new state, route and successor commit together. Identical consumed delivery is acknowledged from retained exact bytes; changed whitespace is a changed payload.], failure: [Early unsolicited values are not buffered. Wrong/stale contract, conflicting duplicate, cancelled wait and malformed value are command refusals. Failed decoding/acceptance does not consume a signal.])

]
#subsection(title: "Absolute deadlines and arming")[
#answers(title: "Wait deadline", responsibility: [Bound one admitted signal, job, child or fork using a restart-stable backend time domain.], interface: [operation.with_deadline(run.After(Duration) or Infinity); seven days is default for new waits. Activities use body timeout instead and cannot declare wait deadlines.], interactions: [Admission and approval precede clock sampling. Arming commits an absolute due time before publishing the wait or creating a managed child. Recovery preserves it. Discovery makes overdue waits eligible after downtime.], invariants: [Time is UTC Unix milliseconds from the lease/discovery clock. Clock errors never fall back on another node clock. Wall-clock correction may advance or delay eligibility; samples need not be monotonic.], failure: [Expiration closes routing first, retains its cause and settles owned work. It cannot undo an effect. Time samples and revision commits are separate: a commit may cross the due time after its last eligibility sample.])

]
#behavior(title: "A duplicate consumed signal is acknowledged once", area: "Signal delivery", level: "interface")[#given[A reference has a committed receipt for a delivered encoded value.] #when[The identical reference and exact bytes are delivered again.] #then[The existing receipt is acknowledged.]#then[Acceptance and successor scheduling do not run again.]]
#behavior(title: "Expiration suppresses late business routing", area: "Wait deadlines", level: "interface")[#given[An admitted wait reaches its retained due time.] #when[A delivery, observation, reconciliation or recovery attempts acceptance.] #then[Expiration records its cause rather than accepting a new business route.]#then[Already known results and unresolved owned cleanup remain retained.]]
#adr(5)

])
#section(title: "Managed children and family budgets", lead: "A durable attachment grants ownership without blocking on another run.", body: [
A checked #term("term-attachment") establishes child ownership. One #term("term-family") can contain both controllers and share retained #term("term-family-grant") values.

#subsection(title: "Managed agent and subgraph")[
#answers(title: "Child attachment", responsibility: [Compose an ordinary agent or subgraph as typed graph work while retaining its own durable record and lifecycle.], interface: [graph.as_subgraph and graph/agent.Definition/new/as_operation expose native input/output adapters. Parent references distinguish agent action, graph activation and fork member.], interactions: [Parent reserves child identity and attachment before start; child retains the reciprocal parent. Lost start acknowledgement adopts the reserved id after identity, initial input and parent checks. Lost completion wakeup reads committed child state.], invariants: [Child creation uses the same store; its business effects pass current policy and bounded ancestry checks. Existing child keeps its native controller. Parent cannot call a waiting child completed or settled merely because it parked.], failure: [Missing reserved child can still be created. Missing acknowledged child is data loss and cannot be recreated. Incompatible definitions, mapping failure, child approval and uncertainty preserve separate states.])

]
#subsection(title: "Ancestry, cancellation and direct handles")[

#points([Before model admission, tool fences, graph effect fences and child starts, verify exact saved attachment through at most 64 ancestry links. A closed, unrelated, unreadable or excessive chain grants no new business work.], [Parent cancellation records intent, cancels admitted children and inserts a terminal never-started tombstone for a reserved absent child. It retains ownership until settled; approval and reconciliation cannot reopen a closed ancestor.], [A parent can park and release its runner and lease while a child waits. Local notifications are hints; discovery observes changed child revisions, including all unsettled fork members.], [Terminal child effects remain evidence. Explicit stored settlement can update a finished parent's history without resuming execution; child uncertainty must be resolved at the child before parent acceptance.], [Legacy missing exact root walks the parent chain. The topmost readable ancestor fallback emits root_inferred and is never persisted as an exact root.])
#answers(title: "Child deadline cleanup", responsibility: [Close parent acceptance at the attachment deadline while preserving the actual child outcome.], interface: [Retained due time, child identity, stop reason and ChildSettled or ChildUnresolved disposition.], interactions: [Expiration records cause before requesting cancellation and uses already reserved cleanup authority. Observation may settle after due time without rearming the business wait.], invariants: [Completion that loses the parent deadline race remains evidence with no parent route. Once a stop cause commits, cleanup does not require another deadline clock sample.], failure: [Uncertain effects retain family and dependency-only discovery. Missing child or incompatible binding refuses settlement rather than fabricating absence or another child.])

]
#subsection(title: "Root reservation ledger")[
#answers(title: "Family budget", responsibility: [Bound cumulative work admissions, child starts and nesting across mixed agent/graph families.], interface: [budget.limits(work:), with_children and with_depth; agent/graph with_family_budget declares limits only on a root. Limits allow zero to deny new work; depth is at most 63.], interactions: [One separate CAS ledger prevents quota writes changing the execution revision. Graph attempts reserve before policy; agent models reserve before call; agent tools reserve at their fence; managed child identity/depth reserve before creation.], invariants: [Exact repeated claim adopts its grant without another charge, even at capacity. New model attempts, replay attempts and cycle visits spend capacity. Failed, unused and uncertain grants are not refunded implicitly.], failure: [Unconfirmed grants release nothing. Changed child depth conflicts. Missing or corrupt initialized ledger and changed limits fail closed. Five conflict retries end as contention, not unlimited capacity.])

]
#points([Root bootstrap stores declaration initialized=false, creates or adopts the ledger, then confirms initialized=true before execution. An initialized missing ledger cannot be replaced. Cancellation during bootstrap seals bookkeeping without restarting work.], [Only roots declare a family budget. Descendants inherit through saved ancestry and cannot reset limits or depth. Budgeted starts require agent writer v7 even for graphs that may later create agents.], [Work counts admission, not token charges, global service load or concurrent body slots. Cleanup consumes existing obligations rather than fresh business grants.])
#adr(5)
#behavior(title: "A repeated grant does not create fresh capacity", area: "Family admission", level: "interface")[#given[A logical work or child claim is already in the root ledger.] #when[The exact claim is retried after acknowledgement loss or recovery.] #then[The original grant is acknowledged.]#then[Usage does not increase again.]#then[Changed identity meaning or child depth is refused.]]

])
#section(title: "Structured forks and joins", lead: "Children own private state; the parent joins retained results in declared order.", body: [
A #term("term-fork") fixes membership before dispatch and retains private child outcomes.


