#section(title: "Persistence compatibility and operational limits", lead: "Versioned bytes carry data; compatible deployed code supplies meaning.", body: [
#subsection(title: "Record envelopes")[

#md-table(3, ([*Envelope*], [*Read / write contract*], [*Retained meaning*], [fabric.run], [Reader accepts versions 1–8; configurable writer 2–8, current 8], [Transcript and initial-input boundary, assistant provider data, actions, approvals, budgets, mixed parents, child settlement and exact family root. An older writer refuses data it cannot represent.], [fabric.graph], [Reader accepts 5–15; current writer 15], [Manifest, initial/state/answer values, ordered receipts, phases, signals, job ownership, deadlines, child/fork scope and family declaration. Old no-deadline records keep none.], [Family ledger], [Independent versioned budget record], [Validated monotonic claims and immutable limits; initialized root marker distinguishes retryable bootstrap from lost data.]))
#points([Formats validate tags, counters, contiguous receipts, route linkage, phase identities, approval correlation and version-dependent fields. A future envelope returns unsupported rather than best-effort reinterpretation.], [Legacy classifier receipts and provider replay turns decode offline under compatible retained operation definitions. Live credentials and strict new transport limits are never historical decoding inputs.], [Rolling deployments choose an agent writer that every deployed reader supports and refuse capabilities older writers cannot represent. Graph format compatibility is independent; downgrading agent writer does not downgrade graph records.])
#points([Agent v8 retains the initial-input boundary. Versions 1–7 infer one original UserMessage for a started run and zero for an empty terminal record. Empty v1–2 cancelled children retain their never-started interpretation; supported v3–7 empty terminal records retain their exact terminal outcome. Their readers remain supported; their writers refuse imported history rather than silently discarding its meaning.], [Recovery validates the initial boundary and its transcript prefix before using it for input equality, generated-message extraction or answer-attempt accounting. Unsupported future versions remain unsupported.])
#adr(6) #adr(13)

]
#subsection(title: "Operations and global capacity")[

#points([Run-local concurrency and family cumulative work bounds do not provide global service admission/backpressure. Applications bound simultaneous requests, callback captures, HTTP pools and external capacity.], [Handles retain current context and bound closures. A handle is not a process or durable credential; releasing it does not release application resources or cancel stored work.], [Leased deadlines, approval expiry and schedules use backend wall clock; BEAM timer durations and callback timeouts use local runtime timing. These cannot be merged into an unstated whole-run deadline.], [A readiness report includes backend reachability, acceptance and lease evidence. The operations runbook distinguishes admission readiness, recovery lag, unresolved effects, upgrade metadata refresh and shutdown accounting.])

]
#subsection(title: "Telemetry delivery")[
#answers(title: "Package observations", responsibility: [Emit package-owned facts about accepted actions, lifecycle, leases, graph progress, recovery and drain.], interface: [fabric/telemetry event descriptors with typed metadata/measurements, run correlation and root. Sinal direct delivery or explicitly configured forwarder supplies observation route.], interactions: [Execution commits precede observations. A synchronous subscriber runs in emitter process and can delay a runner; a bounded best-effort forwarder changes that latency/drop tradeoff.], invariants: [Event failure cannot make a report acceptable again or roll back execution. Full action identity remains durable; turn/call subset is not globally unique. Event ordering across parent/child processes is not a total order.], failure: [Observer blocking can cause RunnerBusy for commands; a withdrawn command cannot apply later. Drops and root inference have explicit events. Telemetry cannot drive child completion, scheduling or business authorization.])

]

])
#section(title: "Verification and extension obligations", lead: "Public consumer behavior, deterministic failures and oracle provenance support claims.", body: [
#subsection(title: "Evidence boundaries")[

#points([Pure controllers test transitions without OTP, clocks or networking. Record suites test meaningful field combinations, malformed state, legacy fixtures and migration refusal. Neither alone establishes durable effects.], [Public runtime tests exercise both sides of effect fences, partial batches, lost acknowledgements, contested answers, cancellation/completion races, lease loss, drain and recovery. Leased memory provides deterministic ownership probes; PostgreSQL adapter scenarios prove its independent backend.], [Separate consumers complete common tasks, advanced settings, caller-owned types and failure handling through public imports. Negative graph authoring fixtures prove incompatible native connections fail to compile.], [The retained check.py gate verifies recipe text equality, package inventory, dependency availability and deterministic consumer/service/experiment suites. It removes known live-provider credentials from child environments and does not load local credential files.], [BeamWeaver is a partial oracle, not the full product specification. Exact fork SHA, license, normalized fixture provenance, deliberate anti-oracles and recapture rules remain in docs/ORACLE.md and test/oracle.])
#adr(9) #adr(10) #adr(11)

]
#subsection(title: "Backend and callback extension")[
#answers(title: "Extension port acceptance", responsibility: [Permit variable providers, tools, verifiers, jobs, stores and graph operations while retaining common lifecycle guarantees.], interface: [Public model function, typed tool binding, versioned operation, approver verifier and backend records/leased functions. Backend conformance exposes deterministic contract checks.], interactions: [Each implementation is exercised from an independent package with a real native domain type/error and meaningful failure handling. Custom providers receive the same admission and budget obligations as built-in adapters.], invariants: [An extension may vary business behavior while Fabric retains fencing, correlation, current context, bounded callbacks and cleanup. Captured resources belong to application lifetime and cannot be serialized.], failure: [Extension authors must truthfully classify possibly-sent effects, version codec changes, bound foreign/network callbacks and preserve unreported measurements. A passing compile proves types, not every operational guarantee.])

]
#subsection(title: "Retained experiments and application evidence")[

#points([experiments/graph_authoring retains synchronous native authoring and external negative consumers; production runtime authority remains in Fabric.], [experiments/workflow_composition retains three composition comparisons and a prototype patch as evidence; it is not a supported runtime dependency or a published Saga capability.], [Agent-recipe counterexample remains executable in test/fabric/support/agent_recipe.gleam. It rejects unsupported action lifecycles and supplies no production replacement guarantee.], [The writing application retains a frozen corpus, artifact tools and sanitized raw captures. Review quality and runtime routing are separately assessed; historical comparison rationale belongs to #adr(12).])

]

